G6a-12 fixed local-task registration and observation reporting, 2026-09-08
Base commit: ff2e6fe (G6a-9), branch dev.
Working-tree base: uncommitted G6a-10 standalone and G6a-11 static multi-task work.
Environment: local Darwin; Linux amd64 cross-compilation only.

Scope:
- Optional named-task reporter -> HTTP -> Server SQLite observations.
- Stable task/syncer/node/identity bindings survive process and database reopen.
- No task dispatch, execution authorization, lease, cold-write fencing, automatic
  assignment, failover, Agent deployment or Gateway request-path change.
- No remote deployment, real Redis/Kvrocks multi-backend test, long stability
  test, throughput measurement, full lag alarm or Gateway protection claim.

Protocol/storage coverage:
- Per-syncer machine credentials; JWT cannot replace them, missing tokens deny.
- Viewer-authenticated runtime GET; bounded report payload and fixed categories.
- Atomic multi-task first registration; conflict rolls back all new bindings.
- Persistent owner/identity checks across reboot and stale observations.
- Sequence/content deduplication without freshness renewal for retained boots.
- Concurrent out-of-order reports, bounded boot history and sticky binding count.
- Duplicate live/stopping boots visible as conflict; terminal state protection.
- Observations do not alter Proxy snapshot version or topology generation.
- Historical boot pruning is not permanent anti-replay protection or fencing.

Reporter/service coverage:
- Stable syncer ID, new boot ID on restart, task identity digest canonicalization.
- Same named tasks restart against SQLite without replacing recorded bindings.
- Final reports follow all task joins; reporter is joined before Service returns.
- HTTP failures do not stop task execution; requests have bounded timeout/body.
- Correct ack sequence required; 401/409/503, malformed and oversized replies.
- Redirects never forward credentials; transport ignores environment proxy.
- Private token files: reject FIFO/symlink/hardlink/public/oversized/multiline.
- Construction does not read tokens; check-config reads but never contacts Server
  or creates journals. Actual startup rechecks credentials before task execution.
- Legacy/local-only metrics preserved: report metrics registered only when enabled.

Fixture limitations:
  Reporter persistence/restart test uses real HTTP and SQLite with fake sync tasks.
  Server router/auth/storage is tested separately against the shared report type.
  This does not test real database replication, data resume or distributed takeover.
  Existing CLI child-process lifecycle tests still use controlled RESP fixtures
  with no RDB/business writes; they are not real Redis/Kvrocks acceptance runs.

